          <h2
            id="guide-title"
            class="mt-4 text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
          >
            Visual Reaction Time: How Fast Can the Human Nervous System
            Respond?
          </h2>

          <p
            class="mt-6 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300"
          >
            Reaction time is not one single biological number. It is the
            combined latency of sensing a stimulus, recognizing what matters,
            selecting a response, sending a command through the nervous system,
            moving a muscle, and registering the physical input on a device. A
            browser reaction-time test therefore measures the complete
            sensorimotor loop rather than the speed of one isolated nerve.
          </p>
        </div>

        <article
          class="mt-12 grid gap-14 text-base sm:text-lg leading-relaxed text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-300"
        >
          <section aria-labelledby="neurobiology-title">
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400"
            >
              01 · Physiology
            </p>

            <h3
              id="neurobiology-title"
              class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
            >
              The physiology of reaction speed
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-5">
              A visual reaction begins when photons strike the retina. Rods and
              cones convert incoming light into changes in membrane potential
              through phototransduction. This is a biochemical process, so
              vision has an early processing cost before an action potential
              can travel down the optic nerve. Visual signals then pass through
              several relay and processing stages, including the lateral
              geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus and primary visual cortex
              (V1) in the occipital lobe. From there, distributed attention and
              action-selection networks help determine that the stimulus is the
              one that should trigger a response.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              The simplified pathway can be pictured as
              <strong class="text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
                retina → optic nerve → LGN → V1 → attention and action
                networks → motor cortex → corticospinal pathways → spinal motor
                neurons → finger muscles → input device
              </strong>.
              Large myelinated axons can conduct impulses at roughly 100 m/s
              under favorable conditions, but that impressive number is only
              one part of the total delay. Synapses, sensory transduction,
              network decisions, muscle activation, and the device pipeline all
              add time.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Auditory reaction time is often faster than visual reaction time
              in simple-response experiments. In the ear, sound-driven motion
              in the cochlea mechanically deflects stereocilia and rapidly
              changes hair-cell receptor currents. The visual system, by
              comparison, must complete phototransduction before the signal
              leaves the retina. Values around 140–170 ms for simple auditory
              responses and around 200–300 ms for simple visual responses are
              useful broad reference ranges, but individual experiments produce
              different numbers.
            </p>

            <div
              class="mt-7 rounded-3xl border border-blue-200 dark:border-blue-900 bg-blue-50/70 dark:bg-blue-950/25 p-6 sm:p-7"
            >
              <p
                class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-blue-700 dark:text-blue-300"
              >
                Important measurement note
              </p>

              <p class="mt-3">
                This test is a performance benchmark, not a clinical
                neurological examination. A percentile here is a statistical
                comparison to the model used by this page; it is not a
                diagnosis, a fitness-to-drive decision, or proof of
                professional-level reflexes.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="benchmark-title">
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400"
            >
              02 · Population context
            </p>

            <h3
              id="benchmark-title"
              class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
            >
              Global benchmark and population percentiles
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Reaction-time literature uses different equipment, stimuli,
              instructions, and inclusion criteria, so a single worldwide table
              should be treated as illustrative rather than normative. The
              rows below are deliberately framed as broad benchmark bands for
              simple visual-response tasks, not as laboratory norms for every
              population.
            </p>

            <div
              class="mt-7 overflow-x-auto rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800"
            >
              <table
                class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm sm:text-base"
              >
