          <section aria-labelledby="hardware-title">
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400"
            >
              03 · Hardware latency
            </p>

            <h3
              id="hardware-title"
              class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
            >
              Display, mouse, and touchscreen latency can move the score
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-5">
              An online reaction time test does not measure biology in a vacuum.
              The observed number includes the time between the browser
              rendering a visible change and the device reporting the user's
              input. Refresh rate is especially easy to understand: a 60 Hz
              display presents a new refresh every 16.67 ms, a 144 Hz display
              every 6.94 ms, a 240 Hz display every 4.17 ms, and a 360 Hz
              display every 2.78 ms. Higher refresh rates therefore reduce the
              maximum frame interval, although actual end-to-end latency also
              depends on scan-out position, rendering, synchronization, and
              the display itself.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Mouse polling is another component. A 125 Hz USB polling interval
              corresponds to 8 ms between polls in the idealized worst-case
              interval model; 1000 Hz corresponds to 1 ms. These are not the
              same thing as total input latency, because device firmware, USB
              scheduling, OS event delivery, browser event dispatch, and the
              physical click all add additional timing variation.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Touchscreens can be more variable still. Digitizer sampling,
              touch filtering, OS gesture recognition, browser processing,
              compositing, and display refresh can all add delay. It is
              reasonable to see tens of milliseconds of additional latency on
              some mobile setups, but a universal 40–75 ms penalty is too
              strong: devices differ, browser implementations differ, and some
              modern phones are substantially faster than others.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-7 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
              <div
                class="rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 p-6"
              >
                <h4
                  class="font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
                >
                  Refresh interval
                </h4>

                <p class="mt-2">
                  60 Hz → 16.67 ms · 144 Hz → 6.94 ms · 240 Hz → 4.17 ms · 360
                  Hz → 2.78 ms.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div
                class="rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 p-6"
              >
                <h4
                  class="font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
                >
                  Polling interval
                </h4>

                <p class="mt-2">
                  125 Hz → 8 ms · 500 Hz → 2 ms · 1000 Hz → 1 ms, before the
                  rest of the input pipeline is counted.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="aging-title">
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400"
            >
              04 · Time of day and aging
            </p>

            <h3
              id="aging-title"
              class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
            >
              Chronobiology, sleep, and age change reaction speed
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Reaction performance is sensitive to arousal and circadian timing.
              In many laboratory settings, response speed improves from the
              sleepy part of the morning into the afternoon, although the peak
              depends on chronotype, sleep schedule, light exposure, caffeine
              use, and the task. A late-afternoon testing window is therefore a
              reasonable practical choice for some people, but it is not a
              universal biological rule.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Age is another strong influence. Simple reaction time tends to be
              fastest in young adulthood and then gradually increases with age.
              The change is not explained by one mechanism. Slower sensory
              processing, changes in attention, reduced motor speed, altered
              neuromuscular recruitment, and age-related neural changes all
              contribute. A claim such as “exactly +2–6 ms per decade” can be a
              useful educational approximation, but individual trajectories are
              wider than that single formula suggests.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Sleep loss can have a large effect on vigilance and response
              stability. Severe sleep deprivation has produced impairment
              levels that researchers have compared with alcohol-related
              impairment, but the exact equivalence depends on the task,
              duration of wakefulness, and study design. Rather than memorizing
              a single “BAC equivalent,” the practical lesson is simpler: do
              not judge your normal reflexes from a score taken when you are
              acutely sleep deprived.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="optimization-title">
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400"
            >
              05 · Tactical optimization
            </p>

            <h3
              id="optimization-title"
              class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
            >
              How to improve a reaction time test score without chasing
              artifacts
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-5">
              The most useful goal is not to force an impossibly small number;
              it is to reduce avoidable variability. Keep your gaze soft and
              centered on the arena instead of staring rigidly at one pixel. A
              relaxed focal strategy can help you notice the global luminance
              change without adding unnecessary eye movements. Keep the
              responding hand in a neutral position with the finger close to
              the input target, but do not pre-click or press against the
              switch.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Anticipatory motor priming can make simple responses quicker
              because the motor system can prepare a likely movement before the
              stimulus arrives. The trade-off is false starts: preparation
              should stop short of guessing the exact trigger time. Consistent
              posture, the same device, the same browser, and the same viewing
              distance are often more valuable than changing hardware between
              trials.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-7 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3">
              <div
                class="rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 p-6 bg-white dark:bg-zinc-900"
              >
                <h4
                  class="font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
                >
                  Soft focal gaze
                </h4>

                <p class="mt-2">
                  Keep the whole arena in view and avoid unnecessary saccades.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div
                class="rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 p-6 bg-white dark:bg-zinc-900"
              >
                <h4
                  class="font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
                >
                  Motor priming
                </h4>

                <p class="mt-2">
                  Rest the responding finger near the control without preloading
                  a click.
                </p>
              </div>

              <div
                class="rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 p-6 bg-white dark:bg-zinc-900"
              >
                <h4
                  class="font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
                >
                  Repeat consistently
                </h4>

                <p class="mt-2">
                  Use the same device and conditions so changes reflect
                  performance, not hardware.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Practice can help, but the improvement is often task-specific.
              Repeating this exact test can reduce familiarity and strategy
              costs, while broader games may improve attention or response
              selection without producing the same gain on a different test.
              Sleep, physical activity, and healthy visual conditions are
              foundational; stimulants should not be used as a substitute for
              adequate rest.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              One of the most useful training principles is consistency. Run
              several sessions at roughly the same time of day, use the same
              browser, display, mouse or phone, and sit at the same distance.
              Browser timers are not hard real-time instruments: the operating
              system can briefly pause a tab, the browser can prioritize other
              work, and a refresh cycle can shift when a visual change becomes
              available. The five-trial average reduces some random noise, but
              it cannot remove those sources of latency completely.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              Eye movements also matter. A soft focal gaze keeps the target area
              inside peripheral vision while minimizing unnecessary saccades. In
              a simple color-change task, the goal is not to “hunt” for the
              green signal but to maintain readiness across the whole arena.
              Pre-motor preparation can shorten the final motor step, yet
              excessive tension can make the click less reliable. The best
              competitive posture is therefore alert but relaxed: eyes centered,
              hand supported, finger near the control, and no deliberate
              prediction of the trigger time.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="measurement-title">
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400"
            >
              06A · Measurement quality
            </p>

            <h3
              id="measurement-title"
              class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
            >
              How to make an online reaction test more repeatable
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-5">
              A good benchmark is repeatable before it is impressive. Keep the
              arena fully visible, close CPU-heavy background tabs, avoid
              changing zoom between attempts, and use a stable pointer or touch
              target. Browser timers are not hard real-time instruments: the
              operating system can briefly pause a tab, the browser can
              prioritize other work, and a refresh cycle can shift when a visual
              change becomes available. The five-trial average reduces some
              random noise, but it cannot remove those sources of latency
              completely.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              That is why your personal baseline is often the most useful
              comparison. Test under one setup, record the average, then retest
              later under the same setup. A change that survives several
              sessions is more meaningful than a single unusually fast score.
              This approach also makes it easier to separate genuine attention
              changes from device effects.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section aria-labelledby="interpretation-title">
            <p
              class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400"
            >
              06 · Interpreting your result
            </p>

            <h3
              id="interpretation-title"
              class="mt-3 text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
            >
              What do reaction time percentiles actually mean?
            </h3>

            <p class="mt-5">
              FreeIQExam uses a Gaussian reference model centered at 265 ms
              with a standard deviation of 42 ms to turn the five-trial average
              into a percentile-style ranking. A lower measured time maps to a
              higher percentile because faster response is treated as better
              performance. This is a mathematical comparison, not a claim that
              the world population truly follows a perfect normal distribution
              with those exact parameters.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-5">
              For the same reason, a 95th-percentile result on this page does
              not mean “you are scientifically proven to be faster than 95% of
              all humans.” It means your score falls around the upper 5% of the
              reference distribution used by the calculator. Use the number as
              a game-like benchmark, and compare it with your own previous
              results under matched conditions.
            </p>

            <div
              class="mt-7 rounded-3xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-zinc-900 p-6 sm:p-7"
            >
              <h4
                class="font-bold text-xl text-zinc-950 dark:text-white"
              >
                Why five trials?
              </h4>

              <p class="mt-3">
                Single trials are noisy. Five valid observations provide a more
                stable average while keeping the test short enough for casual
                use. Because false starts are discarded, the five values in the
                final summary always represent valid green-trigger responses
                rather than penalty attempts.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

